Week 4 — Initial System Configuration & Security Implementation
← Week 3 | Week 4 | Week 5 →
Overview
Week 4 focuses on the initial configuration of the server and implementing foundational security controls. This phase establishes secure remote administration via SSH, sets up firewall rules, manages users and privileges, and ensures that all configurations are documented with evidence for assessment purposes.
All server configuration is performed remotely via SSH from the Ubuntu Desktop workstation.
Objectives
Configure SSH with key-based authentication and disable password login
Implement firewall rules restricting access to the server
Create a non-root administrative user with least-privilege access
Verify remote administration and system configuration
Capture evidence of all configurations and operations
SSH Configuration
Steps Taken:
Generated SSH key pair on the workstation:
ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "studentID@ubuntu-workstation"
Copied the public key to the server:
ssh-copy-id -i ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub user@192.168.56.103
Disabled password authentication and root login:
sudo nano /etc/ssh/sshd_config
# Set:
# PasswordAuthentication no
# PermitRootLogin no
Restarted SSH service:
sudo systemctl restart ssh
Evidence:
SSH connection verified from workstation to server using key-based authentication
Screenshot showing terminal with username@server-hostname prompt
Firewall Configuration
Ubuntu Server firewall (UFW) was configured to restrict access only to the workstation.
Firewall Rules Implemented:
sudo ufw default deny incoming
sudo ufw default allow outgoing
sudo ufw allow from 192.168.56.102 to any port 22 proto tcp
sudo ufw enable
sudo ufw status verbose
Evidence:
Firewall status screenshot (ufw status verbose)
Verified SSH connectivity remains functional
Attempted access from non-authorized IP blocked
User Management and Privilege Enforcement
Created a non-root administrative user:
sudo adduser adminuser
sudo usermod -aG sudo adminuser
Removed or disabled unnecessary default users
Verified privileges with sudo -l:
sudo -l -U adminuser
Evidence:
Screenshots of user creation and privilege verification
Commands executed via SSH from workstation
